export const EXAMPLE_DOCS_PAGE_SOURCES: Record<string, string> = {
  "api-create-note.mdx": "---\ntitle: \"Create a note\"\ndescription: \"Creates a note and returns it with its identifier.\"\nkeywords: [\"post\", \"notes\", \"rest-api\"]\nicon: server\napiMethod: \"POST\"\napiPath: \"/v1/notes\"\n---\n\nCreates a note and returns the stored note with its identifier. Use the **Try it** button to send a test request from your browser.\n\n<ApiEndpoint method=\"POST\" path=\"/v1/notes\" baseUrl=\"https://api.example.com/v1\">\n\n<ApiRequestExample client=\"curl\" lang=\"bash\" label=\"Create a note\">\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"https://api.example.com/v1/notes\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer sk_test_123\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"body\":\"Ship the docs today.\"}'\n```\n\n</ApiRequestExample>\n\n<ApiRequestExample client=\"node-fetch\" lang=\"js\" label=\"Create a note\">\n\n```js\nconst response = await fetch(\"https://api.example.com/v1/notes\", {\n  method: \"POST\",\n  headers: {\n    Authorization: \"Bearer sk_test_123\",\n    \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n  },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ body: \"Ship the docs today.\" }),\n});\n```\n\n</ApiRequestExample>\n\n<ApiResponseExample status=\"201\" contentType=\"application/json\">\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"note_123\", \"body\": \"Ship the docs today.\", \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01T12:00:00Z\" }\n```\n\n</ApiResponseExample>\n\n<ApiResponseExample status=\"400\" contentType=\"application/json\">\n\n```json\n{ \"code\": \"invalid_body\", \"message\": \"The body field is required.\" }\n```\n\n</ApiResponseExample>\n\n<ApiParams title=\"Body\" detail=\"application/json\">\n  <ApiParam name=\"body\" type=\"string\" location=\"body\" required>\n    The text of the note.\n  </ApiParam>\n</ApiParams>\n\n<ApiParams title=\"Response\" detail=\"application/json\">\n  <ApiParam name=\"id\" type=\"string\" location=\"response\" required>\n    The identifier of the note.\n  </ApiParam>\n  <ApiParam name=\"body\" type=\"string\" location=\"response\" required>\n    The text of the note.\n  </ApiParam>\n  <ApiParam name=\"createdAt\" type=\"string\" location=\"response\" required>\n    The time that the note was created, in ISO 8601 format.\n  </ApiParam>\n</ApiParams>\n\n</ApiEndpoint>\n",
  "api-overview.mdx": "---\ntitle: API overview\ndescription: Authentication, base URL, and conventions for the example Notes API.\nkeywords: [\"api\", \"authentication\", \"rest\"]\nicon: server\n---\n\nThis section shows how an API reference looks in Doxa. It documents a small example API for notes.\n\n<Note title=\"Base URL\">\n  All requests go to `https://api.example.com/v1`.\n</Note>\n\n## Authentication\n\nSend your API key in the `Authorization` header of each request:\n\n```bash\ncurl https://api.example.com/v1/notes \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer sk_test_123\"\n```\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Request and response bodies use JSON.\n- Timestamps are ISO 8601 strings in UTC.\n- Identifiers are opaque strings, for example `note_123`.\n\n## Errors\n\nErrors return an HTTP status code and a JSON body:\n\n```json\n{ \"code\": \"not_found\", \"message\": \"Note not found.\" }\n```\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| ------ | ------- |\n| `400` | The request body is not valid. |\n| `401` | The API key is missing or not valid. |\n| `404` | The resource does not exist. |\n",
  "code-blocks.mdx": "---\ntitle: Code blocks\ndescription: Show code with syntax highlighting, file titles, line numbers, and highlighted lines.\nkeywords: [\"code\", \"syntax highlighting\", \"snippets\"]\nicon: code\n---\n\nCode blocks have syntax highlighting and a copy button. Put the language name after the opening fence.\n\n## A basic code block\n\n```bash\nnpm install @example/sdk\n```\n\n## Add a file title\n\nAdd `title=\"...\"` after the language to show a file name above the code.\n\n```ts title=\"src/client.ts\"\nimport { createClient } from \"@example/sdk\";\n\nexport const client = createClient({\n  apiKey: process.env.EXAMPLE_API_KEY,\n});\n```\n\n## Line numbers and highlighted lines\n\nAdd `showLineNumbers` to number the lines. Add line ranges in braces, for example `{3-5}`, to highlight lines.\n\n```ts {3-5} showLineNumbers\nimport { client } from \"./client\";\n\nconst note = await client.notes.create({\n  body: \"Ship the docs today.\",\n});\n\nconsole.log(note.id);\n```\n\n## The same example in more than one language\n\nUse tabs when readers choose one option, for example a package manager:\n\n<Tabs defaultValue=\"npm\" className=\"pt-5 pb-1\">\n  <TabsList>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"npm\">npm</TabsTrigger>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"pnpm\">pnpm</TabsTrigger>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"yarn\">yarn</TabsTrigger>\n  </TabsList>\n  <TabsContent value=\"npm\">\n\n```bash\nnpm install @example/sdk\n```\n\n  </TabsContent>\n  <TabsContent value=\"pnpm\">\n\n```bash\npnpm add @example/sdk\n```\n\n  </TabsContent>\n  <TabsContent value=\"yarn\">\n\n```bash\nyarn add @example/sdk\n```\n\n  </TabsContent>\n</Tabs>\n",
  "components.mdx": "---\ntitle: Callouts and cards\ndescription: Use notes, cards, steps, and tabs to make pages easier to scan.\nkeywords: [\"components\", \"notes\", \"cards\", \"steps\", \"tabs\"]\nicon: layers\n---\n\nComponents add structure that plain Markdown cannot give. In Write mode, type `/` to add a component.\n\n## Notes\n\nUse a note for information that readers must not miss. There are four types:\n\n<Note title=\"Note\">\n  Use the default type for helpful information.\n</Note>\n\n<Note title=\"Success\" type=\"success\">\n  Use the success type to confirm that a task is complete.\n</Note>\n\n<Note title=\"Warning\" type=\"warning\">\n  Use the warning type when an action has a side effect.\n</Note>\n\n<Note title=\"Danger\" type=\"danger\">\n  Use the danger type when an action can cause data loss.\n</Note>\n\n## Cards\n\nUse cards to link to related pages. Put cards in a `CardGrid`.\n\n<CardGrid>\n  <Card\n    title=\"Code blocks\"\n    description=\"Syntax highlighting and file titles.\"\n    href=\"/writing/code-blocks\"\n  />\n  <Card\n    title=\"Diagrams\"\n    description=\"Flowcharts and file trees.\"\n    href=\"/writing/diagrams\"\n  />\n  <Card\n    title=\"Small card\"\n    icon=\"alignJustify\"\n    variant=\"small\"\n    description=\"A compact card with an icon.\"\n    href=\"/publishing/navigation\"\n  />\n</CardGrid>\n\n## Steps\n\nUse steps for a procedure that readers do in order.\n\n<Step>\n  <StepItem title=\"Create an API key\">\n    Open **Settings** and click **New API key**.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Save the key\">\n    Copy the key into your environment file.\n\n```bash title=\".env\"\nEXAMPLE_API_KEY=sk_test_123\n```\n\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Make a request\">\n    Send your first request. The [API reference](/api) shows each endpoint.\n  </StepItem>\n</Step>\n\n## Tabs\n\nUse tabs when readers choose one option from a set.\n\n<Tabs defaultValue=\"macos\" className=\"pt-5 pb-1\">\n  <TabsList>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"macos\">macOS</TabsTrigger>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"windows\">Windows</TabsTrigger>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"linux\">Linux</TabsTrigger>\n  </TabsList>\n  <TabsContent value=\"macos\">\n    Install with Homebrew: `brew install example`.\n  </TabsContent>\n  <TabsContent value=\"windows\">\n    Install with winget: `winget install example`.\n  </TabsContent>\n  <TabsContent value=\"linux\">\n    Install with the script: `curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.sh | sh`.\n  </TabsContent>\n</Tabs>\n",
  "diagrams.mdx": "---\ntitle: Diagrams and file trees\ndescription: Draw flowcharts and sequence diagrams with Mermaid, and show folder layouts with file trees.\nkeywords: [\"mermaid\", \"diagram\", \"file tree\"]\nicon: compass\n---\n\nDiagrams explain how parts of a system connect. Doxa draws them from text, so they stay easy to change.\n\n## Flowchart\n\n<Mermaid\n  chart={`graph LR;\n    Write[Write a page] --> Draft[Save a draft];\n    Draft --> Review[Preview the change];\n    Review --> Publish[Publish];\n    Publish --> Live[Live site];`}\n/>\n\n## Sequence diagram\n\n<Mermaid\n  chart={`sequenceDiagram\n    participant App\n    participant API\n    participant Database\n    App->>API: POST /v1/notes\n    API->>Database: Insert note\n    Database-->>API: Note row\n    API-->>App: 201 Created`}\n/>\n\n## File tree\n\nUse a file tree to show the layout of a project.\n\n<FileTree>\n  <Folder name=\"my-app\" defaultOpen>\n    <Folder name=\"src\" label=\"Source code\" defaultOpen>\n      <File name=\"index.ts\" label=\"Entry point\" />\n      <File name=\"client.ts\" />\n    </Folder>\n    <File name=\"package.json\" />\n    <File name=\"README.md\" />\n  </Folder>\n</FileTree>\n",
  "formatting.mdx": "---\ntitle: Text and formatting\ndescription: Use standard Markdown for headings, emphasis, lists, tables, links, and quotes.\nkeywords: [\"markdown\", \"formatting\", \"tables\", \"lists\"]\nicon: file\n---\n\nDoxa supports standard Markdown and GitHub-flavored Markdown. This page shows the most common elements.\n\n## Emphasis and inline code\n\nWrite **bold text**, _italic text_, and `inline code`. Use inline code for file names, commands, and values, for example `pnpm install` or `docs/index.mdx`.\n\n## Links\n\nLink to another page with a relative path, for example [Quickstart](/quickstart). Link to another site with a full URL, for example [doxa.so](https://doxa.so).\n\n## Lists\n\nUse a numbered list when the order is important:\n\n1. Write the first draft.\n2. Ask a teammate to read it.\n3. Publish the page.\n\nUse a bulleted list when the order is not important:\n\n- Guides explain how to do a task.\n- Concepts explain how something works.\n- Reference pages list every option.\n\nUse a checklist to track work:\n\n- [x] Write the introduction\n- [ ] Add a code example\n- [ ] Publish the page\n\n## Tables\n\n| Option | Default | Description |\n| ------ | ------- | ----------- |\n| `port` | `3000` | The port of the local server. |\n| `open` | `false` | Opens the browser when the server starts. |\n| `logLevel` | `info` | The minimum level of log messages. |\n\n## Quotes\n\n> Good documentation answers the question that the reader has right now.\n\n## Headings\n\nUse `##` for the main parts of a page and `###` for subsections. Each heading appears in the table of contents on the right side of the page.\n\n### A subsection\n\nSubsections appear indented in the table of contents.\n",
  "introduction.mdx": "---\ntitle: \"{{projectName}}\"\ndescription: \"Example documentation that shows what a Doxa site can do. Explore it, edit it, and make it your own.\"\nkeywords: [\"introduction\", \"overview\", \"example\"]\nicon: book\n---\n\nWelcome to your new documentation site. Each page in this example shows one part of Doxa. Read the page, then open it in the editor to see how it is written.\n\n<Note title=\"This is example content\">\n  Change or delete any page. When you connect a GitHub repository and run an AI generation, Doxa replaces these pages with documentation written from your source code.\n</Note>\n\n## Explore the example\n\n<CardGrid>\n  <Card\n    subtitle=\"Start\"\n    title=\"Quickstart\"\n    description=\"Edit a page and publish your first change in three steps.\"\n    href=\"/quickstart\"\n  />\n  <Card\n    subtitle=\"Write\"\n    title=\"Text and formatting\"\n    description=\"Headings, lists, tables, links, and quotes.\"\n    href=\"/writing/formatting\"\n  />\n  <Card\n    subtitle=\"Write\"\n    title=\"Callouts and cards\"\n    description=\"Notes, card grids, steps, and tabs.\"\n    href=\"/writing/components\"\n  />\n  <Card\n    subtitle=\"Reference\"\n    title=\"API reference\"\n    description=\"An endpoint page with request and response examples.\"\n    href=\"/api\"\n  />\n</CardGrid>\n\n## How this site is organized\n\nThis site has two sections. Use the tabs at the top of the page to move between them.\n\n| Section | What it contains |\n| ------- | ---------------- |\n| Documentation | Guides that explain concepts and tasks. |\n| API Reference | One page for each API endpoint. |\n\nInside a section, the sidebar puts pages into groups. This section has three groups: **Get Started**, **Writing Content**, and **Publishing**.\n",
  "navigation.mdx": "---\ntitle: Navigation and sections\ndescription: Organize pages into sections, groups, and a sidebar that readers can scan.\nkeywords: [\"navigation\", \"sidebar\", \"sections\", \"groups\"]\nicon: list\n---\n\nGood navigation helps readers find a page without search. Doxa gives you three levels.\n\n| Level | Where it appears | Example in this site |\n| ----- | ---------------- | -------------------- |\n| Section | Tabs at the top of the page | Documentation, API Reference |\n| Group | Headings in the sidebar | Get Started, Writing Content |\n| Page | Items in the sidebar | Quickstart, Code blocks |\n\n## Change the navigation\n\nIn the dashboard, open **Site settings** and then **Navigation**. There you can:\n\n- Add, rename, and remove sections.\n- Move pages between groups.\n- Set an icon or a badge on a page. The **Diagrams and file trees** page in this site has a **New** badge.\n\n## Tips for a clear sidebar\n\n1. Put the pages for new readers in the first group.\n2. Keep each group to about seven pages or fewer.\n3. Use short page titles. The sidebar has little space.\n\n<Note title=\"Site appearance\">\n  Change the colors, logo, and fonts of your site in **Site settings** and then **Appearance**.\n</Note>\n",
  "publish.mdx": "---\ntitle: Publish and connect GitHub\ndescription: Publish your changes, and connect a GitHub repository to write docs from your source code with AI.\nkeywords: [\"publish\", \"github\", \"ai generation\"]\nicon: globe\n---\n\nYour site is live from the moment that you create the project. Each time that you publish, Doxa makes a new version of the site.\n\n## Publish a change\n\n<Step>\n  <StepItem title=\"Edit pages\">\n    Make your changes in the editor. Doxa saves them as a draft.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Review the draft\">\n    Use **Split** mode to preview each page before you publish.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Publish\">\n    Click **Publish**. Readers see the new version in a few seconds.\n  </StepItem>\n</Step>\n\n## Write docs from your code with AI\n\nDoxa can read your source code and write the documentation for you. This feature needs a GitHub repository.\n\n<Step>\n  <StepItem title=\"Connect a repository\">\n    Open the project overview and click **Connect repository**. Install the Doxa GitHub App, then select a repository.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Run a generation\">\n    Click **Generate docs**. Doxa reads the repository and writes a full documentation site.\n  </StepItem>\n</Step>\n\n<Note title=\"Generation replaces this example\" type=\"warning\">\n  The first AI generation replaces all the pages of this example site with pages written from your code.\n</Note>\n",
  "quickstart.mdx": "---\ntitle: Quickstart\ndescription: Edit a page, preview it, and publish your first change.\nkeywords: [\"quickstart\", \"edit\", \"publish\"]\nicon: rocket\n---\n\nUse these steps to make your first change to this site.\n\n<Step>\n  <StepItem title=\"Open the editor\">\n    In the dashboard, open **Content**. Select this page, **Quickstart**, in the page list.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Change the text\">\n    Click in the page and type. Use **Write** mode for a visual editor, or **Split** mode to edit the MDX source next to a live preview.\n  </StepItem>\n\n  <StepItem title=\"Publish\">\n    Click **Publish**. Your change goes live on your site in a few seconds.\n  </StepItem>\n</Step>\n\n<Note title=\"Your changes are saved\" type=\"success\">\n  Doxa saves every edit as a draft. Nothing changes on your live site until you publish.\n</Note>\n\n## Edit in the editor or in MDX\n\nEvery page is an MDX file. MDX is Markdown with components. Choose the mode that you like:\n\n<Tabs defaultValue=\"write\" className=\"pt-5 pb-1\">\n  <TabsList>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"write\">Write mode</TabsTrigger>\n    <TabsTrigger value=\"mdx\">MDX source</TabsTrigger>\n  </TabsList>\n  <TabsContent value=\"write\">\n    Write mode works like a document editor. Use the toolbar or type `/` to add headings, lists, code blocks, and components.\n  </TabsContent>\n  <TabsContent value=\"mdx\">\n\n```mdx title=\"quickstart/index.mdx\"\n---\ntitle: Quickstart\ndescription: Edit a page, preview it, and publish your first change.\n---\n\nUse these steps to make your first change to this site.\n```\n\n  </TabsContent>\n</Tabs>\n\n## Next steps\n\n- Learn the Markdown basics in [Text and formatting](/writing/formatting).\n- Add notes, cards, and steps with [Callouts and cards](/writing/components).\n- Change the sidebar and sections in [Navigation and sections](/publishing/navigation).\n",
};
